+
+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edit Policy
+
+## Context
+
+A visit can be edited on more than one tablet, or on one tablet and the server, before either copy reaches the server. Each copy starts from some revision of the visit and changes scientifically meaningful fields: observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Two edits from the same starting revision can change unrelated fields (an observer name and a missing species) or related fields (a species identification and its cover estimate). The record store must decide what the current record of a visit is when such edits arrive, and must do so without discarding any submitted edit.
+
+The policy determines what field workers see when their edit conflicts, what coordinators review, and which visits can enter the partner's Friday export. Tablet-sync and review-export will be sized around it, so a change later is expensive. The record store cannot infer scientific relationships between fields from field names, so any automatic combination is a rule the team must maintain.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates**
+
+- *Inherited:* every original submission is retained with its author and receipt time. An option that loses a submitted edit is eliminated. (HLD tenet: do not silently discard a submitted edit.)
+- *Doc-local:* the current visit state can be explained as a function of retained submissions and recorded resolutions. An option whose current state cannot be traced to its inputs is eliminated.
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+
+- **Scientific integrity of combined visits — major.** Tenet: keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable. Combining fields automatically can produce a visit no worker submitted, where two individually valid changes together break a relationship between fields the rules do not model.
+- **Coordinator review workload — major.** Tenet: prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator. Review load is the human cost of the option; it grows with the number of visits edited concurrently.
+- **Risk of unread resolution — major.** Not named by the HLD tenets; a latent criterion. A review queue large enough to be routinely cleared by approving whichever revision is listed first would reproduce the silent loss the policy exists to prevent. This criterion cuts against the option that produces more review items.
+- **Offline capture and tablet storage — moderate.** Tenet: let field workers continue collecting observations without connectivity. Each option changes what a tablet must keep while disconnected and what it must send on reconnection.
+- **Rule maintenance burden — moderate.** Automatic combination requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, and tests that show the rules hold.
+- **Export readiness — moderate.** Share of visits that can enter the Friday export without a coordinator decision.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each submission names the revision it started from. The server accepts the submission as a complete visit revision. When a submission names a revision that is no longer current, it is stored as a competing revision alongside the current one; it does not overwrite anything, and it is not merged. Until a coordinator resolves the competing revisions, the visit has no single accepted current state, and the retrieval response says so. A resolution produces a new revision that records the coordinator's identity and the submission IDs it draws from.
+
+Demonstration: visit V (revision 4) is edited on tablet A to change the observer list and on tablet B to change the cover estimate for one species. Both submissions name revision 4. The server stores revision 5 from A and a competing revision 5 from B. The coordinator compares them and resolves to revision 6, which keeps A's observers and B's cover estimate.
+
+- *Gate, doc-local (current state traceable):* **passes.** The current state is either a single accepted revision or a recorded resolution over named submissions.
+- *Major — scientific integrity of combined visits:* **strong.** No visit is produced that no worker submitted, except by an explicit coordinator resolution, which is recorded.
+- *Major — coordinator review workload:* **weak.** Unrelated edits to one visit create review items the same as related edits do.
+- *Major — unread resolution risk:* **weak.** The review queue grows with concurrent edits, including unrelated ones. Mitigation depends on the review interface showing the field-level difference.
+- *Moderate — offline capture and tablet storage:* **partial.** The tablet keeps the base revision ID and its own pending edit. It needs no base snapshot, but it must show competing revisions when it reconnects.
+- *Moderate — rule maintenance burden:* **strong.** No field-level merge rules exist to maintain.
+- *Moderate — export readiness:* **weak.** Visits with unresolved competing revisions are excluded from the export set until resolved.
+
+Summary: keeps each submission whole and reviewable, at the cost of more review work.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each submission carries its base state and its structured field changes. The server applies changes whose fields do not overlap with the current state automatically, producing a combined revision. Changes to the same field, or to fields the rules mark as related, become a review item. Original submissions are retained in both cases; the combined revision is derived from them.
+
+Demonstration: the same two edits as above. The observer change and the cover-estimate change touch disjoint fields, so the server stores revision 5 combining both. No coordinator sees the visit. If the second edit had instead changed the species identification, the rules would need to know whether that identification and the cover estimate are related before deciding whether to combine them.
+
+- *Gate, doc-local (current state traceable):* **passes.** Each combined revision is derived from named submissions and rule versions.
+- *Major — scientific integrity of combined visits:* **weak.** Automatic combination produces visits no worker submitted. Whether the result is scientifically consistent depends on rules that cannot see relationships outside the fields they name.
+- *Major — coordinator review workload:* **strong.** Disjoint edits never reach a coordinator.
+- *Major — unread resolution risk:* **partial.** Fewer items reach review, so the queue is less likely to be cleared unread. Items that do reach review are overlap cases, and the reviewer must still see the field-level provenance to judge them.
+- *Moderate — offline capture and tablet storage:* **weak.** Each tablet keeps a base snapshot per visit and sends structured changes. Storage and reconnection logic are larger.
+- *Moderate — rule maintenance burden:* **weak.** Rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries must be written, tested, and maintained as the team's data model changes.
+- *Moderate — export readiness:* **strong.** Most visits reach export without coordinator action.
+
+Summary: reduces review volume, at the cost of rules that can combine related edits without anyone noticing.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions. The major criteria on scientific integrity and coordinator workload favor the two options differently, and the deciding one is integrity. A visit that a coordinator reads as a whole is one the team can explain to the science partner. A combined visit that no worker submitted is harder to explain, and the explanation depends on rules that cannot see every scientific relationship. Field-aware merging wins on workload and export readiness; those are real costs, and they are the costs accepted here. The unread-resolution criterion is the one that could reverse this choice (see below).
+
+**What committing forecloses.** Automatic combination of visit fields inside the record store's current-state derivation. Adding it later is a new decision, and it would need its own rule set, which the scientific-integrity criterion would have to accept.
+
+**What committing obligates downstream.**
+
+- *Record-store (this node):* the submission envelope carries the starting revision ID. Retrieval distinguishes an accepted current revision from a visit with unresolved competing revisions. Resolution creates a new revision and records the coordinator's identity and contributing submission IDs (REC-006).
+- *Tablet-sync (cascade note; its design is not yet written):* each tablet keeps the base revision ID of every local edit and retains its pending edit until the server acknowledges it. On reconnection, the tablet shows each competing revision the worker's edit conflicts with and what the server now holds. The tablet does not merge fields on the worker's behalf. It must retain the submitted payload for the full offline period, since a worker may need it after a competing revision has been resolved.
+- *Review-export (cascade note; its design is not yet written):* the coordinator interface needs a revision comparison view that shows, for each competing revision, the field-level difference from the others. The export set excludes any visit with unresolved competing revisions and reports the excluded visits so coordinators know what is missing from Friday's file. The export contains only accepted revisions.
+
+**Conditions under which this should be revisited.** Measure these over the first season rather than estimating now:
+
+- Coordinators are routinely resolving competing revisions without changing the chosen revision, or the review queue is cleared faster than the comparison view can be read. This indicates the unread-resolution risk has materialized.
+- The number of visits with unresolved competing revisions at Friday export regularly prevents the partner's file from covering most plots. This indicates the export-readiness cost is unacceptable.
+
+Either finding reopens this decision against the same criteria. Changing it would be a new decision, not an amendment to this one.
